eu environment ministers have agreed a  common line on new rules for countries  to restrict or prohibit the cultivation  of genetically modified organisms on  their territories even if they're  authorized at european level currently  the european commission approves the  cultivation of gmos on the basis of  scientific advice and in consultation  with the european food safety authority  or fsa if the commission and efsa say no  to cultivation  it will be a no to everyone  if the commission and deficit say yes  it won't be a yes  to everyone and on specific grounds  5 10 15 20 member states can block the  cultivation on their territory the aims  to provide a legal basis to limit gmo  cultivation on grounds other than health  or environmental considerations  these could include socioeconomic  reasons land use and town planning or  agricultural and public policy  objectives  the incoming italian presidency plans to  start negotiations on the gmo package  with the newly elected european  parliament in autumn  while they're widely grown in the  americas and asia genetically modified  crops are generally unpopular in europe  environmentalists have raised concerns  about the impact on biodiversity  europe's clear air program was also up  for discussion at the council in  luxembourg the program sets limits on  harmful emissions from industry traffic  energy plants and agriculture  the ministers debated new rules on  medium-sized combustion plants which are  used for residential heating and cooling  and providing heat or steam for industry
 

